{"slots": {"question": [{"claim": "The study asks how the Democratic Party's spreading of stigmatizing labels such as 'convicted criminal' about Trump affected Democrats' perceptions of people with criminal records, and how it affected Democrats' sense of being represented by their own officials.", "quotes": [{"text": "Thus, this study explores what may have been the impact of propagating such labels on Democrats’ perceptions of people with criminal records, and to what extent does the propagation of such stigmatizing terminology impact how well Democrats perceive to be represented by their own elected officials?", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "It also asks whether a counterexample to Trump can offset the advertisement's possible negative effects. The counterexample is the perspective of a formerly system-involved representative on the label.", "quotes": [{"text": "Additionally, this study seeks to understand whether providing a counterexample to Trump – highlighting the perspective of a formerly system-involved representative on the propagation of this label by the Democratic party – may counter-act potential negative impacts of the Party’s Trump advertisement.", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "The paper argues against the stigmatizing framing itself. Acknowledging Trump's convictions was necessary, but the use of stigmatizing terms was not.", "quotes": [{"text": "Though acknowledgement of Trump’s convictions by the Democratic Party was likely unavoidable and in fact, necessary, the use of stigmatizing terms was not.", "page": 2}]}], "central_claim": [{"claim": "Exposure to the 'convicted criminal' Trump advertisement significantly worsened Democrats' perceptions of people with criminal records, while improving their perceived feelings of representation.", "quotes": [{"text": "Results suggest that exposure to the Trump advertisement with the “convicted criminal” label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records, but improved perceived feelings of representation.", "page": 1}]}, {"claim": "A counter-stereotype narrative from a formerly system-involved representative, shown before the ad, largely eliminated the ad's negative effect on perceptions of people with records.", "quotes": [{"text": "Findings suggest that the counter-stereotype vastly ameliorated the negative effects of the advertisement on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with criminal records.", "page": 1}]}, {"claim": "The author recommends that the party consider and test messaging that elevates justice-involved voices instead of relying on stigmatizing attacks.", "quotes": [{"text": "utilizing political messaging that elevates dedicated, justice-involved voices within the party rather than rely on stigmatizing attacks should be strongly considered and tested.", "page": 1}]}], "evidence": [{"claim": "The design is a pre-registered survey experiment with three arms, assigned roughly evenly across 2,439 participants: control, Trump ad alone, and counter-narrative first.", "quotes": [{"text": "Thus, the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the Control group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).", "page": 4}, {"text": "All data and code, as well as the pre-registration and pre-analysis plan for this study are available via OSF.", "page": 6}]}, {"claim": "The sample was recruited on Prolific in July 2025. It consisted of fluent-English-speaking U.S. residents who were Democratic Party members.", "quotes": [{"text": "2,500 participants were recruited for this study, between July 12th and 13th of 2025 [60].", "page": 8}, {"text": "This survey was fielded amongst individuals residing within the United States who spoke fluent English and were members of the Democratic Party.", "page": 8}]}, {"claim": "The ad stimulus was Trump's mugshot labeled 'CONVICTED CRIMINAL,' as used in 2024 advertisements. The counter-narrative was a lightly edited excerpt from a Nevada Current article about a formerly incarcerated lawmaker.", "quotes": [{"text": "specifically President Donald J. Trump’s mugshot, which was labeled, in all capital letters, “CONVICTED CRIMINAL.”", "page": 5}, {"text": "This excerpt was from the article entitled, “Could a formerly incarcerated lawmaker reshape justice policy in the NV Legislature?,” by Michael Lyle, published in the Nevada Current on November 5, 2024 [55].", "page": 5}]}, {"claim": "Outcomes were Likert-based measures, mostly standardized indices, covering six areas: perceptions of people with records, three representation dimensions, officials' motives, and ad support.", "quotes": [{"text": "The primary outcomes of interest in this study were: a) perceptions of individuals with criminal records; b) perceived value of representation; c) perceived quality of Democratic representation in federal government; d) complexity of representation; e) motives of elected officials; and f) advertisement support.", "page": 7}]}, {"claim": "Average treatment effects were estimated with least squares regressions using HC-2 robust standard errors. Models were run both with and without pre-specified covariates.", "quotes": [{"text": "To estimate each ATE, least squares regressions with heteroskedasticity-consistent standard error estimators, HC-2, were utilized.", "page": 10}]}], "key_findings": [{"claim": "The Trump ad alone significantly reduced positive perceptions of people with criminal records compared with no ad.", "quotes": [{"text": "Both H2A and H2B were supported, namely, exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records", "page": 11}]}, {"claim": "Showing the counter-narrative before the ad raised positive perceptions of people with records by more than 0.8 SD compared with the ad alone. This almost entirely offset the ad's harm.", "quotes": [{"text": "exposure to the counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump advertisement increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviation (SD) units, compared to those presented with the Trump advertisement alone", "page": 11}, {"text": "First, there is clear evidence that the Trump advertisement caused decreases in positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records amongst Democrats, but when paired with (and preceded by) a counter-narrative of a dedicated, system-impacted state representative who speaks to the harm of the “criminal” label, these effects were almost entirely ameliorated", "page": 15}]}, {"claim": "The ad increased feelings of being well represented and the belief that officials are public-service motivated. The author suggests that Trump serves as a reference point that makes other officials look comparatively better.", "quotes": [{"text": "Additionally, exposure to the Trump advertisement significantly increased feelings of being well represented by elected officials and the extent to which Democrats perceived that elected officials were motivated by public service over self-interest.", "page": 15}, {"text": "Collectively, these results may simply be Prospect Theory in action: Donald J. Trump may be serving as reference point that may influence Democrats to see other elected officials as comparatively better [73].", "page": 15}]}, {"claim": "Compared with the ad alone, the counter-narrative reduced support for the ad and worsened perceived quality of Democratic representation.", "quotes": [{"text": "Starting with H1A, exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement, relative to the Trump advertisement alone", "page": 11}, {"text": "providing the counter-narrative also worsened perceptions of the quality of Democratic representation, relative to the Trump advertisement alone.", "page": 15}]}, {"claim": "In supplemental analyses, the ad was more damaging to perceptions of people with records among system-impacted respondents.", "quotes": [{"text": "the Trump advertisement exposure appeared to be significantly more damaging to perceptions of individuals with criminal records for those who are system-impacted.", "page": 14}]}], "scope_and_limits": [{"claim": "The study is presented as preliminary evidence only.", "quotes": [{"text": "this work should be considered as preliminary evidence of potential effects of the Democratic Party’s messaging strategy, and a line of work that warrants far more empirical attention than it has received.", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "The design was not full factorial and had no counter-narrative-only arm. The effect of the counter-narrative alone therefore cannot be identified.", "quotes": [{"text": "First, the ideal version of this survey experiment would have been to utilize a full factorial design (i.e., including the exposure to the counter-narrative perspective arm alone).", "page": 16}, {"text": "the counter-narrative arm cannot be directly compared to the pure control arm to test the impact of the counter-narrative alone because it also involves exposure to the Trump advertisement.", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "The effect of the mugshot image cannot be separated from the effect of the stigmatizing label.", "quotes": [{"text": "it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from the stigmatizing terminology imposed, and thus, this is recommended for exploration in future work.", "page": 17}]}, {"claim": "Because the sample is an online panel, generative AI or bot responses are a concern.", "quotes": [{"text": "this survey was fielded amongst an online panel of survey respondents, and the proliferation of generative AI should cause concerns over the humanity of the responses provided within the survey.", "page": 17}]}, {"claim": "Hawthorne effects and social desirability bias cannot be ruled out.", "quotes": [{"text": "it could be the case that participants were subject to the Hawthorne effect, resulting in outcomes being driven – in some capacity – by social desirability bias.", "page": 17}]}, {"claim": "Several questions remain open: whether the effects last, whether repeated exposure changes them, whether they translate into behavior rather than self-reports, and whether they hold during a campaign. The study was fielded after the election.", "quotes": [{"text": "a) for how long do the effects of the counter-narrative prime before the advertisement and the advertisement itself persist", "page": 17}, {"text": "to what extent would these treatments translate to shifted decision-making and behavior, as opposed to self-reported perceptions and attitudes?", "page": 17}, {"text": "Importantly, when such studies are implemented will be important, as this current study was conducted in July of 2025, after the outcome of the election was known and not actively during the campaign.", "page": 17}]}, {"claim": "The perceptions index had only modest reliability, and better measures are needed.", "quotes": [{"text": "given the reliability statistics for the perceptions of individuals with criminal records index was satisfactory but not necessarily strong, utilizing a more robust set of measures is an important next step.", "page": 18}]}, {"claim": "The study cannot rule out that the intervention creates a new category of 'deserving' people rather than reducing stigma.", "quotes": [{"text": "While it is possible that the intervention constructs a new category of deservingness, rather than reducing stigma, the empirical relationship between attitudes and personal connection suggests that reduction of stigma is more likely [55–57].", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "The motives finding (H1F) does not survive a Bonferroni correction.", "quotes": [{"text": "the significance of H1F’s test would no longer hold after application of a (Bonferroni) multiple comparisons correction.", "page": 11}]}, {"claim": "Racial attitudes were measured only as a pre-treatment covariate, so the study cannot show whether the ad shifts racial attitudes.", "quotes": [{"text": "racial attitudes were measured as covariates and presented before stimuli", "page": 18}]}, {"claim": "The paper does not claim that Democrats should have ignored Trump's conviction.", "quotes": [{"text": "this is not to say that failing to acknowledge or address the criminal conviction of Donald J. Trump would have been advised.", "page": 15}]}], "theoretical_positioning": [{"claim": "The author extends social categorization theory to political advertising. The prediction is that labeling a salient exemplar (Trump) spills over onto the whole category of people with records.", "quotes": [{"text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such labels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.", "page": 1}, {"text": "Thus, in this context, once the label of ‘felon’ and ‘convicted criminal’ was applied to Donald J. Trump – and done so quite routinely and saliently – theory and evidence would predict greater homogenization and spillover perceptions from Trump", "page": 2}]}, {"claim": "The author builds on the perspective-getting literature, applying it as a campaign-messaging intervention. The paper extends that literature to representation outcomes, which it says are understudied there.", "quotes": [{"text": "Alternatively, one presumably unexplored messaging intervention in the 2024 election – though heavily supported by political science and psychological literature – is perspective-getting [42–44].", "page": 3}, {"text": "Replication of this work with a full factorial design is strongly encouraged, especially since outcomes related to representation quality are far more limited in the perspective-getting literature.", "page": 17}]}, {"claim": "The author combines the affective and policy-alignment accounts of representation, proposing messaging that addresses both.", "quotes": [{"text": "well as one that signals their policy alignment (i.e., dedication to justice system reform), would be well-suited to addressing both the affective and policy representation avenues for bolstering support.", "page": 4}]}], "interlocutor_claim": [{"claim": "Prior labeling research (refs 8–10: Denver et al.; Harris & Socia; Imhoff) found that the 'criminal' label causally worsens perceptions compared with person-first language. The author builds on this to predict spillover from labeling Trump.", "quotes": [{"text": "Extant literature highlights the causal impact of propagation of labels, such as ‘criminal’, to significantly worsen perceptions of individuals with records, relative to more humanizing, person-first language", "page": 2}]}, {"claim": "A body of work (refs 30–32) holds that emotional appeals build political support through in-group ties and affective polarization. The author qualifies this by pointing to research that policy alignment is crucial to feelings of representation.", "quotes": [{"text": "A considerable body of evidence suggests that invoking an emotional response may encourage political support, which may downplay focusing on representation regarding policy positions, leaning instead into heightening in-group ties and fostering affective polarization [30–32].", "page": 3}, {"text": "However, this is not necessarily concrete, in that alignment of policy standpoints has been shown to be crucial to feelings of representation [34–36].", "page": 3}]}, {"claim": "Evidence attributed to ref 54 (Martinez) suggests that incarceration shifts ideology rightward. The author questions whether this would hold without stigmatizing status-quo messaging.", "quotes": [{"text": "Though some evidence suggests that experiencing incarceration may serve to shift ideologies further to the right [54], whether that would remain true outside of status quo political messaging (i.e., operationalizing stigmatizing labels of individuals with records) remains unclear.", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "The author builds on meta-analytic evidence (ref 7, Lau et al.) that negative campaign ads do not aid election success, using it to question the negative framing.", "quotes": [{"text": "meta-analytic evidence does not empirically support the use of negative campaign advertisements to aid election success [7].", "page": 2}]}, {"claim": "Work on AI threats to online surveys (e.g., Westwood, ref 62) is characterized as focused mostly on open-text responses. The author uses this to argue that such concerns should be weighed in context for closed-ended surveys.", "quotes": [{"text": "much of the literature that documents the potential threats and/or prevalence of generative AI in online surveys focuses specifically on open-text threats, with some exceptions (e.g., Westwood, 2025) [62].", "page": 17}]}]}}
